## What I did differently, and why

**I did not make one `@future` call per Opportunity.**

A batch of 200 would exceed the limit of 50 future invocations per transaction, fail during the CPQ load, repeat the same queries for the same account, and enqueue unnecessary jobs. Instead, the trigger collects a deduplicated `Set<Id>` and makes **one** `@future` call per transaction. This required changing the service signature to `recalculate(Set<Id> accountIds)`, which is also a valid `@future` parameter type.

Additionally:

- On update, both the old and new `AccountId` are recalculated when an Opportunity is reparented.
- Delete and undelete are handled; otherwise deleting the last open Opportunity would incorrectly leave an account `Hot` or `Warm`.
- If Opportunities are already being saved from a future, batch, or queueable context, the synchronous implementation is used because `@future` cannot be called from those contexts.
- “Open” uses the derived `IsClosed = false`, rather than hard-coding open Stage values.
- Accounts are only updated when their calculated `Rating` actually changes.

File: force-app/main/default/triggers/OpportunityTrigger.trigger
```apex
trigger OpportunityTrigger on Opportunity (after insert, after update, after delete, after undelete) {
    Set<Id> accountIds = new Set<Id>();

    if (Trigger.isDelete) {
        for (Opportunity opp : Trigger.old) {
            if (opp.AccountId != null) {
                accountIds.add(opp.AccountId);
            }
        }
    } else if (Trigger.isUpdate) {
        for (Opportunity opp : Trigger.new) {
            Opportunity oldOpp = Trigger.oldMap.get(opp.Id);

            Boolean isRelevantChange =
                opp.StageName != oldOpp.StageName ||
                opp.Amount != oldOpp.Amount ||
                opp.AccountId != oldOpp.AccountId;

            if (!isRelevantChange) {
                continue;
            }

            if (opp.AccountId != null) {
                accountIds.add(opp.AccountId);
            }

            // Recalculate the previous account if the Opportunity was reparented.
            if (oldOpp.AccountId != null) {
                accountIds.add(oldOpp.AccountId);
            }
        }
    } else {
        // After insert and after undelete.
        for (Opportunity opp : Trigger.new) {
            if (opp.AccountId != null) {
                accountIds.add(opp.AccountId);
            }
        }
    }

    if (accountIds.isEmpty()) {
        return;
    }

    // A @future method cannot be called from a future or batch context.
    // Run synchronously if the Opportunity save is already asynchronous.
    if (System.isFuture() || System.isBatch() || System.isQueueable()) {
        AccountRatingService.recalculateNow(accountIds);
    } else {
        // One bulkified asynchronous call for the entire transaction,
        // rather than one future invocation per Opportunity.
        AccountRatingService.recalculate(accountIds);
    }
}
