**Change explained**

I deliberately did **not** implement one `System.enqueueJob(new CaseSurveyJob(c.Id))` per case. Salesforce allows at most 50 Queueable jobs to be enqueued in one synchronous transaction, so closing 200 cases through the API would exceed that limit, throw an exception, and roll back the case closures.

Instead, the trigger collects all newly closed case IDs and enqueues a single bulkified job. Failure isolation is preserved inside the job by using `Database.insert(tasks, false, AccessLevel.USER_MODE)`, so one invalid Task does not prevent the others from being created. The job also re-queries the cases to confirm they are still closed and still have a contact before creating Tasks. I retained a single-`Id` constructor for compatibility, although the trigger uses the bulk constructor.

